        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          Expedition 69 began on March 28, 2023 with the departure of the Soyuz MS-22 (68S) from the International Space Station (ISS, “the station”). After MS-22 was found to have a coolant leak during Expedition 68, a decision was made to return the spacecraft to Earth unmanned. But the tradition of marking the end of one Expedition and the beginning of another by the departure of the Soyuz spacecraft held.<br><br>Left behind and transferring to Expedition 69 were former MS-22 crewmembers, Sergey Prokopyev (Commander of Expeditions 68 and 69, RSA), Dmitri Petelin (RSA), and Frank Rubio (NASA). Their mission was extended until September when they would return to Earth on the Soyuz MS-23 (69S). Also transferring to Expedition 69 were Stephen Bowen (NASA), Warren Hoburg (NASA), Sultan Alneyadi (United Arab Emirates, UAE), and Andrey Fedyaev (RSA).<br><br>While operating and maintaining the space station's systems, the Expedition 69 crew continued to operate the many science experiments in progress as well as initiate new investigations and undertake technology demonstrations.<br><br>Three NASA spacewalks were planned and completed. On April 28, Bowen and Alneyadi performed a 7-hour spacewalk during which they prepared the station’s starboard truss for the installation of two ISS Roll-Out Solar Arrays (iROSAs) as part of a long-planned upgrade to the station’s power channels. The first four iROSAs were installed during previous Expeditions. Bowen and Hoburg followed up by installing the fifth iROSA during a June 9 spacewalk of about 6 hours and the sixth iROSA during a June 15 spacewalk of about 5-1/2 hours.&nbsp; Five planned RSA spacewalks were also completed.<br><br>The Expedition 69 crew also welcomed visitors to the ISS. The second private mission to the station, Axiom Mission 2 (AX-2) by Axiom Space, launched with a crew of four on a SpaceX rocket on May 21, 2023, docking to the station on May 22. The AX-2 crew lived and worked with the Expedition 69 crew for 8 days, completing twenty experiments before undocking for the return home. The AX-2 splashdown occurred on May 30, 2023.<br><br>The crew rotation in preparation for Expedition 70 began on August 26. SpaceX Crew-7, the seventh operational mission of NASA’s Commercial Crew Program, launched to the ISS with a crew of four including Jasmin Moghbeli (Crew-7 Commander, NASA), Andreas Mogensen (Crew-7 Pilot, ESA), Satoshi Furukawa (JAXA), and Konstantin Borisov (RSA).<br><br>This was followed by undocking and return to Earth of SpaceX Crew-6 carrying crewmembers Bowen, Hoburg, Alneyadi, and&nbsp;Fedyaev on September 2.<br><br>Then, with the September 15 launch and docking of the Soyuz MS-24 (70S), crewmembers Oleg Kononenko (RSA), Nikoli Chub (RSA), and Loral O’Hara (NASA) were welcomed aboard the station and the crew rotation was nearly complete.<br><br>Prokopyev turned command of the station over to Mogensen on September 26, in preparation for the return of the Soyuz MS-23 and the 69S crew to Earth.<br><br>Expedition 69 came to a close on September 27, 2023 when MS-23 undocked for the journey home with crewmembers Prokopyev, Petelin, and Rubio on board .<br><br>The MS-23 crew had completed 371 days in space allowing Rubio to set a record for the longest single spaceflight by an American astronaut.<br>
